project/editor-apps-imperative-filebrowser: spoint EditorApps.js (the editor Apps tab) renders its app list IMPERATIVELY via createElement/append into persistent _toolbarHost + _listHost, NOT through webjsx applyDiff. Reason: webjsx's reconciler drops a `...filtered.map()`-ed .ds-ep-tree-item's SECOND child (the inline file-list expansion div) on re-render — so clicking an app toggled _expApp/selected but the file list never appeared (item.children stayed at 1), and Monaco was unreachable. Isolated test proved kit h() renders 2 children + skips a null middle child fine, so the bug is reconciliation of dynamically-added children in a mapped list, not h() itself. Fix: _appRow() builds caret(.ds-ep-tree-twist '>') | label(.ds-ep-tree-label flex:1) | optional desc tag | .ds-ep-tree-rowbtn Place, and appends the expansion (.ds-ep-tree-children file rows / Loading… / No files) as a real DOM child; renderList() does _listHost.replaceChildren(); setApps/setAppFiles call renderList only (not full render) so the kit SearchInput Filter keeps focus; the Monaco code view (renderEditorPane) still owns the whole container when _curApp&&_curFile. Clicking a file row -> onGetSource -> server SOURCE -> openCode -> Monaco (vs-dark rgb(30,30,30), fills .ds-ep-panel-body flush, Back/Save toolbar). DEBUGGING TRAP that caused many false-negatives: `.ds-ep-tree-item.selected` ALSO matches the SceneHierarchy's currently-selected ENTITY (carries data-eid) in the LEFT panel — always scope Apps-tab probes to `.ds-ep-tree-item[data-app-drag]`. MOBILE (max-width:760px): selecting an entity toggles `.ep-overlay.has-selection` and calls inspectorPane.scrollIntoView() so the selected entity's properties surface; do NOT swap pane `order` to promote the inspector — it overlaps the stacked panes; keep flex:1 bands + scrollIntoView. Witness: cache-disabled reload (CDP Network.setCacheDisabled + bust query) because ES modules cache per-URL; served path is /editor/EditorApps.js (client served at prefix / from sdkRoot/client, StaticHandler mtime-keyed cache).